{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-163222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/63118a7b3d7fd589513f41ae1e721baf25305f480e683c984088fedb9a8d759b.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then over a region $D$ in the $yz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-163222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,\\,:\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type II region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7dbabd349afb41c00c447ab61e28139cc7818a74c54bd17a61eb77d05ae558ae.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,\\,:\\,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4040e2d2eadc5c39b9224b1b39980de486fe6dd69dad1e63179c6cffcb4046b4.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-163222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-163222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-163222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}^{1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{y}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-163222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/9f4f24dd100758466d8a06eee113bada8b1bede4b48c0a3e694e4c1e3f1c2371.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4}-z}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type I region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/dc1a9b6371456f635cd4b73fe99301fa349ceeec1341b9bd3b86f6bcd7ad8ba2.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=1-\\frac{x}{4}-z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{x}{4}-y.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{4}-y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/07c4ee04dfba186348b3bd56b182477cec9ca1a581e37bb0eb4ab9a3d64ee515.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+4x}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1+4x}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/4-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/96b24f85c19f388c152dae265c3794179ebc9e1e6fc06a1fe10f82627b73d29c.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(x,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{2-x}{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-x-2y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type III region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/f08e4fc4d1d2933e9c12fe7df96a1029d66a83c68beff4fa31928cc010554766.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=2-x-2y\\,⟹\\,y=(2-x-z)/2.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{2-x-z}{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2639dbff9d1d4269abb59ccd3eefa0b246c8d1f183fd8f6ef132a41cc07e345d.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-38980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{2-x-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2-z}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d76d928d43054c7d5c026cc25b95fb55855c7a8cbdb5fb35b5ae1f1d39a35c90.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(x,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le (y-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type III region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/116ae6061410bca820b3c89869b23a36b5cef47e4fe483e390ee43d1e52ff726.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=(y-1)^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=1-\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-\\sqrt{z}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5613cb5b9718914e35f7259e3fe9dfd69ce128a861d19995e2c836c2b76d0f58.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(y-1)^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{1-{z}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}-1}^{1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-\\sqrt{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/bd69636822b7a3f68805273a4ffd72c7011a4f945a3ebbac8dcd99191e7ea730.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then over a region $D$ in the $yz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{3-x}{3},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x-3y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type II region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d26a7cc2c643b300226136a223be0fdebed4e17c99c2e562d1cc209caa023ea5.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=3-x-3y\\,⟹\\,x=3-3y-z.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 3-3y-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5641e6fc83054a355a2010084e1c8be2cd2d8144eae2aad0706704247f03b258.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(y-z)/3}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-y+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3}^{3-y+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7094d1527f7feb9dac54cbd90e595dcbb71c11d5c0a32d1371c3c63c54127899.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x.$ The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/c48de52085373361c4e4de3010ebe36c1da702f0efec45b455dff891869086f2.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1-y\\,⟹\\,y=1-z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/fd5d71a6d6509851e5131fe458b9672d59eb7ff6c07499949ae199b45f4ddae0.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1-z \\\\ y=x\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,x=1-z\\,⟹\\,z=1-x.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/12743c492a7f7883a2ecd8d3ca73138a324a53040cb8936f4794f9e8d84c8fd5.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{z}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7c959b9c517b17d9427de450def510b9673d91fd951e28696d4d56d8a768b74d.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ and then $z.$ The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/c3837a3551b35a5146fd2c8e2441e4a9d6d947e8c52e44254d48eb854e54e605.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx={y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/22581a56f4d9cb611bd9a58b06b1036cd47b500217570ae6890d0f38dba6742c.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve given by \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x} \\\\ y=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\sqrt{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/e2305d3da63886703666fd58a2adfe8f15f2eceb7261922f46ba4949b0fc0cde.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ then $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/192cb96645cef0340e99769af3126b9d7e3775ed419e8414e3ad1ed683e919e3.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$ The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z),$ ${u}_{2}(y,z),$ ${v}_{1}(y),$ ${v}_{2}(y),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $R$ as a type II region and its projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/60f61df1520d366f6cbcac0b87e7e5eb453e8f011ff046b29a61cdae41624ddf.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the rear boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=1-x\\,⟹\\,x=1-z.\n$$\n\n- The rear boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 1-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/02bbf0de5dad7ff72970ef5a939ac66fd05dfc06c31a23c37b6204042e1859b8.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $yz$-plane with the top curve given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-z \\\\ x=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{y}{2}=1-z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2eea77ae3d1ae7a459dba98c98b20fabbec6b1d3a6cacb718b32688bd4ad11d7.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-2y}{\\int }_{2y}^{1-z/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{1}^{1+z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-2z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{1}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y/2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{1-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Change the order of integration in the following integral, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ then $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{2},\\,\\,{y}^{3}\\le z\\le 2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type I region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/58a9056015b3dad91ec7ae64bff893c8c6c2cce6480c979545e423cbe105ef03.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${y}^{3}\\le z\\le 2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2fdd6a15b4ddadfc3ee24af5e35e2a87e9e963ceeefbf858b704d78ee0b78513.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/65da730d32f200b8f9da004ee8cf6b7e79095e323961f4a5b6dfa0bddeace42b.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz={y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt[3]{z}\n$$\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/1743f540a85fc1c266c84da8f34b2012516b7bbc98d113cf7c62ba3bba8173c1.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by \n\n$$\nz=(\\sqrt[3]{2})^{3}=2.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b76bf84e1179bec6a1e8e357c6cc628eee95c1663e6f0ff375135612a6f275de.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{3}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{z+1}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{z}^{3}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Change the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type I region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/87b2dd6f4b527f2b709f3e5ede2add7e71d87145c1a15bcc4dbf7066ee41cde1.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/1ad3b88cc426acf67beba4298eb889ce986879b1ae894d5f95ab8d8f3e7b7cf2.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b401109bb512f5ad13ccef3346fd3cf2c51eb70eced978aa995f3db597408196.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/2393b79260273fa658f846ed9013156dbc280195b8d7a0e9b0aeca6cd46e545d.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x \\\\ y=\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,z=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/f14c08f7d809bbb9eb83dc6f34bfd69ed93963c5dab219d7e6fb16720204ae61.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ then $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-x}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{x}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z),$ ${u}_{2}(y,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\frac{z}{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/29c722bce3c23c2af628f18dfaf16695f9ec5beac68b0d16ce93d6a993d6fb8c.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/cdc8fb2c7f6ef9a28b01094ef3bf18089297a6ef9a431c5152311e5e92cd2557.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type II region and its projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d805df26c5a471323d855194b41e052142e711e7f7f9ddf910d792ae74cbc2c6.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4d40e420abeec633cd145dca33071d6459e4b4009e4c6cfc871d6c6150be51e4.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $yz$-plane with the left curve given by $y=\\sqrt{0}=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/81782a6c847e5369dfed7524619de354ff7676a856685fb4340d09cf61dca339.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}{[y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\cdot [{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y] \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0)\\cdot (-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\pi -2"] ["math" "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{1}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/380b44a8baf8ed15eb3eb4e66e77b820f5bc51a73be840526db67d96f6d763ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}y}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/185f85445f95382afdd9516351f0cce3c4d2b11d99f65da2d5a36db0fc853215.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/6aeba17b071bd275be7147ffb925b488881a2fb8de5f544dbffe8caab5c036e4.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}y\\,⟹\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,2x\\le y\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/be089ef60080ad9181db8a5dacccf6abe382bef492369342fa15761134b507d2.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve given by $x=\\frac{1}{2}(2)=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/885b884fee574cae3c2f62ac0092e64b9be1731ae8510f6e910d0a101ffa3f20.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}[{\\int }_{2x}^{2}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2-2x}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2(1-x)}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[z]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-162488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-162488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/8ac5fd57665f292d8a013f47aaf20226944315bb1f74695e895dca40332b28c6.png)\n\nEvaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\nThe region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/30e43d707479d17f45fb74c023d16706ba9308ffc4421cb17a23d7c2ef16fbee.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4429082d4ab0d6fdef75d79b1db02944f0c53741fb0ccf19d8a4ef46a253698f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\n$$\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/a941e1bfabb21561b622080775c3c5389a86cf4150f1fb28dd692a022f607620.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by $z=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/20e339260b0947f92d8fedfa47e899ed53753e03e30c4b816edec06d092d9a01.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}{[y]}_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{e}^{{z}^{3}}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{e-1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-39154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{e-1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{e-1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{3}"] ["math" "e-1"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e-1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,y),$ ${u}_{2}(x,y),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi },\\,\\,{z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/3bb477b93fb92174307bee87130d9957b135cdafaa4167f78f3d603c4192391d.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/cf966e9ec5fbcf11727d46bb3906f05e4b30df4c9bef86a6eab4850b967d7ba0.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/88b5e19c3e263d160db8d041fd3504b25d4a8a8f7ae8e111cdeb5b153c3cd21f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny={z}^{2}\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b748578888fdda878b0d44bb036310644c3437c1922aafd49cab6c165a9cf132.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=\\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/60e35d0e77314aaecc60b0eda3c039afa0bdd44d49f68ad39e27c8856a9390e0.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}){[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{x}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-39415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-39415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/446e61491ae43e610dc39e4b46b752668969540f7733bf7d9c945fa260210a77.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b486ea542dc330a483fa76f333ab80c9fcd07434db5dc0c5a0a62a335294f127.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/b626c20c336bc81df9194f161998f7c6aea524a11495fede615cdf76f5d93f59.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=y\\,⟹\\,y=z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/7386453aebec047469c829e2c2c5d6c4ab915ac965bbcd39838dc7be50941d54.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the bottom curve given by $z=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/4b2bb3583b5c0807c653f503cdf9ed954f38db8a30eef548738e96cedb889288.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{3}{(1-{z}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1ee6b2037d466502e22e5850156eaffcbb2649dcb6b5d59314e290eec939253e"} {"question" "q-38982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "q-38982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7c98b66705af52ffe7e5c59286042d4dfcc8d5e0f818be8ee3139df99108087"} {"question" "e-11675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/a8d3e8c54d61326d9f7d5203d32bd48006c404e9d3990820cb8884c08df8dba3.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/example-11675.json" "file_sha256" "f13d066fdbfb0f10bb3b4d04fa562c5ddf97927aaf5b0f49972bbd0611f6beaf"} {"question" "e-11675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$ and $u_2(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{\n(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{z} \n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type I region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/efc1013a8202f8eea26250b904cf3d4672b30d478ddaad72b6347b274bd33f7d.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt[3]{z}, \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = y^3.\n$$\n\n- The top boundary of the region is $z = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{\n(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: y^3\\leq z \\leq 1\n\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/3ae61409e70d4d6d6227a306ab1651331d9b40633248fd525cefbab519202a52.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/example-11675.json" "file_sha256" "f13d066fdbfb0f10bb3b4d04fa562c5ddf97927aaf5b0f49972bbd0611f6beaf"} {"question" "e-11676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Change the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/example-11676.json" "file_sha256" "4fbb1c062425ec07e7d4073177b5f1d41cfaa4e1b2746509612802d278ffd9ec"} {"question" "e-11676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find limits of integration $u_1(x,y),$ $u_2(x,y),$ $v_1(x),$ $v_2(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first draw the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{\n(x,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1\n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/8007b0f7348f9855a009aff56c676122a739e6210d4f024c524e72e3cfa23312.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $0 \\leq y \\leq  1-z^2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/97d0f5fc56498e1a044dbbfc07da571c1806903822dfdb1d68f877723ce9a559.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/f5fab194951b50a2a0c81aff8d422b98be5145bbef3123ea746b86bcebd1e778.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny = 1-z^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = \\sqrt{1- y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{1-y} \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/403675d42fae7e437e62bcb3e254e3f7cf3997556606ed529807d83253d1c439.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/1e1fbae22c5ad39225a5de4e3c3aeb025cb41b21bbf0c127da57e3ada10c3087.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ then $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{0}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\sqrt{1-y}}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/example-11676.json" "file_sha256" "4fbb1c062425ec07e7d4073177b5f1d41cfaa4e1b2746509612802d278ffd9ec"} {"question" "e-12405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/example-12405.json" "file_sha256" "4b243dce7478353792d295e683afb014cba4bca203704fb8874b7e6e3c7bb5a0"} {"question" "e-12405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y),$ $u_2(x,y),$ $v_1(y),$ $v_2(y),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n=\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} e^{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2, \\:\\: z \\leq x \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/9fe61cd50d31086b47495f0bd0fd71308fc30286d392f4d0064cf743b93272b0.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $z \\leq x \\leq 2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/6af345af2d33b53c698e8e1d4eff10e3b3116911e14ee1b4581da7eee8528f94.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/53ec4e56ca38362b10c05d4cc657f9057005d9692e0fcde1f0846e7e80b4f478.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z = z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\nx = z \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad z = x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5ce67f398f60237d763d393d62f9e7346270b954acc7efdc7a096a5f3155f4d3.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xy$-plane with the left curve given by $x = 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/0dbf7a35fe74730db7c2a07fc4bbe881a66412cee53bf0a606c522f5493e58c2.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{z}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[z]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\cdot [y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/example-12405.json" "file_sha256" "4b243dce7478353792d295e683afb014cba4bca203704fb8874b7e6e3c7bb5a0"} {"question" "q-38980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "2104b5322357a481b792bf092d2ad8903d73dd75752d3bf59bfd25f090144c56" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "The original region is x,y,z≥0 with x+2y+z≤2. Its xz projection is D={(x,z):x≥0,z≥0,x+z≤2}. For each point in D, solving the plane equation for y gives 0≤y≤(2-x-z)/2, yielding original option 0. The worked solution and saved Correct grading confirm this derived judgment. The final solution line contains a typo in the original dy upper bound, but the original question, preceding region description, geometry, and resulting mixed integral consistently establish the answer." "bindings" {"problem" "3d3466cc0870acf4b23c519fb66066abd0675b350e7a66cf3d23c8264900b7c4" "worked_solution" "3f99d9ebae77572f63b2f4ff3696be9c3ece08bf88a7ae84fdc3937e1982618b" "original_field" "97ccd1c245248c4849fe7f34a67b4b053658f36fa803d281c4f552eedbecd983" "reviewed_field" "6de469c4905999a149ada8b940e0c76f31bee4f594a20d84d5703ed481debef2" "raw_widget" "a92ab51861d0fb938c55de48178f79bbe738e59a3ce234280ac0325051ce500e"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-38980-before.png" "sha256" "c08662a8670ee2eade143c393fb5ff0a05fd791f5b13224f13d0f9382619529c"} {"path" "history-q-38980.png" "sha256" "851caaf3f4984e3d03198918713529d7a74e043b6976fc39f0f4bdacd4db20f5"} {"path" "q-38980-after.json" "sha256" "b755d4163850cee4342d800ec096f7749c55d9acd9272e50985366580528561b"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "12c1581683789a97845497b88ea4f463f41e868366d2f76b7d2e45cf5b6d6c18"}]} {"question" "q-38980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{2-x-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"] ["math" "{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2-z}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "2104b5322357a481b792bf092d2ad8903d73dd75752d3bf59bfd25f090144c56" "rationale" "The original region is x,y,z≥0 with x+2y+z≤2. Its xz projection is D={(x,z):x≥0,z≥0,x+z≤2}. For each point in D, solving the plane equation for y gives 0≤y≤(2-x-z)/2, yielding original option 0. The worked solution and saved Correct grading confirm this derived judgment. The final solution line contains a typo in the original dy upper bound, but the original question, preceding region description, geometry, and resulting mixed integral consistently establish the answer."}] "usage" {"e-12405" [] "e-11676" [] "q-38980" [] "e-11675" [] "q-39154" [] "q-39415" []} "basis" 2703 "retractions" [[17592186198887 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/q-38980.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n$∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{(2 - x)/2}∫_{0}^{2 - x - 2y}f(x, y, z)dzdydx$\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y$, then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above."] [17592186198887 :question/answer-fields 17592186197557] [17592186198896 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/q-39154.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}e^{z^{3}}dzdydx$.\nThe region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $dydzdx$.*"] [17592186198897 :question/problem "$∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{π}}∫_{z^{2}}^{\\sqrt{π}}z\\cos (\\frac{π}{6} + y^{2})dydzdx =$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $dzdydx$.*"] [17592186197941 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/dc30042ba8467a4dd3b57e4729cf275e.png)"] [17592186197941 :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$ and $u_2(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z = \\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{z} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}A$, we write $R$ as a type I region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/20ba647fd3e3402dc4ba57a1efe199b3.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y)$, is\n$y = \\sqrt[3]{z}, \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad z = y^3$.\n- The top boundary of the region is $z = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: y^3\\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/54727f66b4a3d3b312c032189ed08a48.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z = \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$"] [17592186197942 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$.\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then $y$, and then $x$."] [17592186197942 :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$, $u_2(x,y)$, $v_1(x)$, $v_2(x)$, $a$, and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first draw the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/0898e2ab1dad983d6db2c113b25a7b9b.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/7bf3173bf663a363a02d25d6025c92dc.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x$, we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/54b1cbed41d7992355278fe388a141c6.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y)$, is\n$y = 1-z^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad z = \\sqrt{1- y}$.\n- The bottom boundary of the region is $z=0$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{1-y} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/5a04ed84a421e394756b01cbdd473210.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/84fb222f81aaade0074f70c558fa3b30.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then $y$, then $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{0}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\sqrt{1-y}}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$"] [17592186197966 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$.*"] [17592186197966 :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$, $u_2(x,y)$, $v_1(y)$, $v_2(y)$, $a$, and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y = \\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} e^{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2, \\:\\: z \\leq x \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/50867af2658a2fd8436bc8f1c49b01d9.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where\n\n$$\nz \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/183a99ad9f325c9c75801f18e667d562.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$, we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/0f5cb134455e0cfceef74be513903e4a.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n$z=0$.\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z = z(x,y)$, is\n$x = z \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad z = x$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/e4de33214185ed8a7bebf8a9db1b1051.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xy$-plane with the left curve given by $x = 0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/8b095ecbfe344bc149ece073098d6a15.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{z}^{2}e^{x^{2}}dxdzdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{x}e^{x^{2}}dzdxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}e^{x^{2}}[∫_{0}^{x}dz]dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}e^{x^{2}}[z]_{0}^{x}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}xe^{x^{2}}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{2}xe^{x^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{2}e^{x^{2}}]_{0}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{1}(e^{4} - 1)dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{4} - 1) \\cdot [y]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{4} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186197558 17592186197559 17592186197560 17592186197561 17592186197562 17592186197612 17592186197613 17592186197614 17592186197615 17592186197616 17592186197618 17592186197619 17592186197620 17592186197621 17592186197622] "immutable_fields" [[17592186197557 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197557 :answer-field/id] [17592186197557 :answer-field/key] [17592186197557 :answer-field/type] [17592186197611 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197611 :answer-field/id] [17592186197611 :answer-field/key] [17592186197611 :answer-field/type] [17592186197617 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197617 :answer-field/id] [17592186197617 :answer-field/key] [17592186197617 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
